{
  "case_id": "2009-08-651",
  "document_hash": "2680d3fef795246349cdf4ba48574b87c70617184e33c0ef1dde61c780711ccd",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2009-08-651",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2680d3fef795246349cdf4ba48574b87c70617184e33c0ef1dde61c780711ccd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2680d3fef795-0000-0005",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 1,
        "start": 1
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The above-entitled matter came on for oral argument before the Supreme Court of the United States at 10:04 a.m."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-08-651",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2680d3fef795246349cdf4ba48574b87c70617184e33c0ef1dde61c780711ccd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2680d3fef795-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Kentucky Supreme Court announced a categorical rule so restrictive of the Sixth Amendment that the United States Government disavows it. The court held that the Sixth Amendment never provides a remedy to a defendant who pleads guilty to a crime on the false advice of his attorney that he would not be deported as a result."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2009-08-651",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2680d3fef795246349cdf4ba48574b87c70617184e33c0ef1dde61c780711ccd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2680d3fef795-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I think, for misadvice, the test would be whether it's a material misrepresentation that would be material to a reasonable defendant in deciding whether to plead guilty, so it would --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2009-08-651",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2680d3fef795246349cdf4ba48574b87c70617184e33c0ef1dde61c780711ccd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2680d3fef795-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Why would it be limited to a decision to plead guilty? What if a decision to plead guilty would have lesser immigration consequences than a guilty verdict after -- after going to trial? Wouldn't you have the same situation there?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2009-08-651",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2680d3fef795246349cdf4ba48574b87c70617184e33c0ef1dde61c780711ccd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2680d3fef795-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: How do you decide which of the many consequences your rule would cover? I mean,"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2009-08-651",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2680d3fef795246349cdf4ba48574b87c70617184e33c0ef1dde61c780711ccd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2680d3fef795-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Well, we can't leave that for another day. I mean, we -- we have to decide whether we are opening a Pandora's box here, whether there is any sensible way to restrict it to -- to deportation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2009-08-651",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2680d3fef795246349cdf4ba48574b87c70617184e33c0ef1dde61c780711ccd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2680d3fef795-0001-0000",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: There is a fundamental difference between Petitioner's claim that defense counsel has a duty to advise his client about all of the myriad collateral consequences that may stem from a criminal conviction, which the government does not think that a defense counsel has under the Sixth Amendment, and the claim that is focused more precisely on misadvice given by defense counsel on a material collateral consequence to a defendant."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2009-08-651",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2680d3fef795246349cdf4ba48574b87c70617184e33c0ef1dde61c780711ccd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2680d3fef795-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 21,
        "start": 21
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Well, then you are saying that the more the defense counsel strays from his only professional responsibility, the more at risk the government is. That seems odd."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2009-08-651",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2680d3fef795246349cdf4ba48574b87c70617184e33c0ef1dde61c780711ccd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2680d3fef795-0001-0002",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The rule right now in 10 Federal circuits is there\u2019s no duty to advise about collateral consequences. Seven Federal circuits have a rule that affirmative misadvice about collateral consequences can support a claim."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2009-08-651",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2680d3fef795246349cdf4ba48574b87c70617184e33c0ef1dde61c780711ccd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2680d3fef795-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 35
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In Hill v. Lockhart, this Court again focused on voluntariness and said that voluntariness of the plea depends on counsel's advice and whether that counsel advice is in the range of competence of the attorneys in a criminal proceeding."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Sotomayor",
      "case_id": "2009-08-651",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2680d3fef795246349cdf4ba48574b87c70617184e33c0ef1dde61c780711ccd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2680d3fef795-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 36,
        "start": 36
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "But how do you do that? I mean, your adversary's argument is, in their particular case -- and I know that you dispute this \u2013- there is a defense that could win at trial."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Breyer",
      "case_id": "2009-08-651",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2680d3fef795246349cdf4ba48574b87c70617184e33c0ef1dde61c780711ccd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2680d3fef795-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE BREYER: What would you do? I'm asking you, would you tell him? He doesn't know about the immigration law. He thinks it's just a year. You yourself have learned that he probably will be killed, as will his family, if he pleads guilty. Would you tell him that?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Long",
      "case_id": "2009-08-651",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "2680d3fef795246349cdf4ba48574b87c70617184e33c0ef1dde61c780711ccd",
      "evidence_id": "ev-2680d3fef795-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 39,
        "start": 39
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. LONG: Well, Your Honor, the -- first of all, the prevailing professional norm or ethical obligations that have been enacted in Kentucky and in most States provide very general obligations and they do not actually speak to this kind of situation."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2009-08-651",
